= #text("Of Smells / 论气味")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("It has been reported of some, as of Alexander the Great, that their sweat exhaled an odoriferous smell, occasioned by some rare and extraordinary constitution, of which Plutarch and others have been inquisitive into the cause. But the ordinary constitution of human bodies is quite otherwise, and their best and chiefest excellency is to be exempt from smell. Nay, the sweetness even of the purest breath has nothing in it of greater perfection than to be without any offensive smell, like those of healthful children, which made Plautus say of a woman:")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("据报道，一些人，例如亚历山大大帝，他们的汗液会散发出一种臭味，这是由某种罕见而非凡的体质引起的，普鲁塔克和其他人一直对其原因感到好奇。但人体的一般构造却完全不同，它们最好、最重要的优点就是没有气味。不仅如此，即使是最纯净的呼吸的甜蜜，也没有什么比没有任何令人厌恶的气味更完美的了，就像健康儿童的气味一样，这使得普劳图斯这样评价一个女人：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("“Mulier tum bene olet, ubi nihil olet.” [“She smells sweetest, who smells not at all.” —Plautus, Mostel, i. 3, 116.]")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("“Mulier tum bene olet，ubi nihil olet。” [“她闻起来最香，谁闻起来一点味道都没有。” ——普劳图斯，莫斯特尔，i。 3、116。]")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("And such as make use of fine exotic perfumes are with good reason to be suspected of some natural imperfection which they endeavour by these odours to conceal. To smell, though well, is to stink:")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("那些使用精美的异国香水的人有充分的理由怀疑他们试图通过这些气味来掩盖一些自然缺陷。闻起来虽然很好，但还是很臭：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("“Rides nos, Coracine, nil olentes Malo, quam bene olere, nil olere.” [“You laugh at us, Coracinus, because we are not scented; I would, rather than smell well, not smell at all.”—Martial, vi. 55, 4.]")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("“骑不，Coracine，nil olentes Malo，quam bene olere，nil olere。” [“你嘲笑我们，科拉西努斯，因为我们没有气味；我宁愿不闻，也不想闻。”——Martial，vi。 55、4。]")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("And elsewhere:")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("以及其他地方：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("“Posthume, non bene olet, qui bene semper olet.” [“Posthumus, he who ever smells well does not smell well.” —Idem, ii. 12, 14.]")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("“死后，non bene olet，qui bene semper olet。” [“Posthumus，闻起来好闻的人闻起来并不好闻。” ——同上，ii。 12、14。]")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("I am nevertheless a great lover of good smells, and as much abominate the ill ones, which also I scent at a greater distance, I think, than other men:")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("尽管如此，我还是一个非常喜欢好气味的人，并且同样厌恶难闻的气味，我认为，我比其他人在更远的地方闻到这些气味：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("“Namque sagacius unus odoror, Polypus, an gravis hirsutis cubet hircus in aliis Quam canis acer, ubi latest sus.” [“My nose is quicker to scent a fetid sore or a rank armpit, than a dog to smell out the hidden sow.”—Horace, Epod., xii. 4.]")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("“Namque sagacius unus odoror、Polypus、aliis Quam canis acer 中的 gravis hirsutiscubet hircus、ubi 最新 sus。” [“我的鼻子闻出恶臭的疮口或臭腋窝的气味，比狗闻出隐藏的母猪的气味还要快。”——Horace，Epod.，xii。 4.]")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("Of smells, the simple and natural seem to me the most pleasing. Let the ladies look to that, for ‘tis chiefly their concern: amid the most profound barbarism, the Scythian women, after bathing, were wont to powder and crust their faces and all their bodies with a certain odoriferous drug growing in their country, which being cleansed off, when they came to have familiarity with men they were found perfumed and sleek. ‘Tis not to be believed how strangely all sorts of odours cleave to me, and how apt my skin is to imbibe them. He that complains of nature that she has not furnished mankind with a vehicle to convey smells to the nose had no reason; for they will do it themselves, especially to me; my very mustachios, which are full, perform that office; for if I stroke them but with my gloves or handkerchief, the smell will not out a whole day; they manifest where I have been, and the close, luscious, devouring, viscid melting kisses of youthful ardour in my wanton age left a sweetness upon my lips for several hours after. And yet I have ever found myself little subject to epidemic diseases, that are caught, either by conversing with the sick or bred by the contagion of the air, and have escaped from those of my time, of which there have been several sorts in our cities and armies. We read of Socrates, that though he never departed from Athens during the frequent plagues that infested the city, he only was never infected.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("在气味中，对我来说，简单和自然的似乎是最令人愉悦的。让女士们看看这一点吧，因为这主要是她们关心的：在最严重的野蛮行径中，斯基泰妇女在沐浴后，习惯于用她们国家种植的某种有气味的药物在脸上和全身涂粉和结痂，这种药物被清洗掉，当她们与男人熟悉时，发现她们芳香四溢、光滑。令人难以置信的是，各种气味都如此奇怪地附着在我身上，而我的皮肤又是多么容易吸收它们。那些抱怨大自然没有为人类提供将气味传送到鼻子的工具的人是没有理由的。因为他们会自己做这件事，尤其是对我；我的大胡子就承担着这个职责；因为如果我用手套或手帕抚摸它们，气味一整天都不会消失。它们在我去过的地方显现出来，在我肆意的年龄里，那些亲密的、甘美的、吞噬的、粘稠的、融化的吻，在我的嘴唇上留下了几个小时后的甜蜜。然而，我发现自己很少受到流行病的影响，这些疾病要么是通过与病人交谈而感染的，要么是通过空气传播而传播的，并且是从我那个时代的流行病中逃脱的，在我们的城市和军队中已经存在过几种流行病。我们读到苏格拉底，虽然他在瘟疫频繁侵袭雅典时从未离开过雅典，但他只是从未被感染。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("Physicians might, I believe, extract greater utility from odours than they do, for I have often observed that they cause an alteration in me and work upon my spirits according to their several virtues; which makes me approve of what is said, that the use of incense and perfumes in churches, so ancient and so universally received in all nations and religions, was intended to cheer us, and to rouse and purify the senses, the better to fit us for contemplation.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("我相信，医生可能比他们从气味中获得更大的效用，因为我经常观察到它们根据它们的几种美德引起我的改变并作用于我的精神；这让我赞同这样的说法，即在教堂中使用焚香和香水如此古老，并为所有国家和宗教所普遍接受，其目的是为了振奋我们，唤醒和净化感官，更好地适合我们沉思。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("I could have been glad, the better to judge of it, to have tasted the culinary art of those cooks who had so rare a way of seasoning exotic odours with the relish of meats; as it was particularly observed in the service of the king of Tunis, who in our days—[Muley-Hassam, in 1543.] —landed at Naples to have an interview with Charles the Emperor. His dishes were larded with odoriferous drugs, to that degree of expense that the cookery of one peacock and two pheasants amounted to a hundred ducats to dress them after their fashion; and when the carver came to cut them up, not only the dining-room, but all the apartments of his palace and the adjoining streets were filled with an aromatic vapour which did not presently vanish.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("我本可以很高兴，更好地判断它，品尝那些厨师的烹饪艺术，他们有一种罕见的方法，可以用肉类的味道来调味异国的气味；但他们的烹饪技巧却是令人难以置信的。这在突尼斯国王的服役中尤为明显，他在我们这个时代——[Muley-Hassam，1543年]——登陆那不勒斯，会见查理皇帝。他的菜肴里涂满了有香味的药物，一只孔雀和两只野鸡的烹饪费用高达一百金币，才能按照它们的风格来烹调。当雕刻师来切割它们时，不仅餐厅，而且他宫殿的所有公寓和毗邻的街道都充满了芳香的蒸汽，而且这种蒸汽并没有立即消失。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("My chiefest care in choosing my lodgings is always to avoid a thick and stinking air; and those beautiful cities, Venice and Paris, very much lessen the kindness I have for them, the one by the offensive smell of her marshes, and the other of her dirt.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("我在选择住所时最关心的始终是避免空气粘稠而恶臭。那些美丽的城市，威尼斯和巴黎，大大减少了我对他们的善意，一个是沼泽地的难闻气味，另一个是她的泥土。")]

